no(241,'Sodium carbonate is the intended soda-lime glass ingredient, but sodium sulfate and sodium nitrate are also used as fining/oxidising additives in glass manufacture. The broad used-in-manufacture wording has more than one correct alternative. Need original main-flux qualifier or a separately authored question, not an unmarked new premise.',answer='B intended main flux; C/D also glass additives')
yes(242,'B','Aluminium is too reactive for its oxide to be reduced commercially with coke. In the Hall–Héroult process, alumina is dissolved in molten cryolite and electrolysed. Aluminium ions gain electrons at the cathode to form aluminium metal.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/18-2-occurrence-and-preparation-of-the-representative-metals'],question='How is aluminium extracted commercially from its ore?',options=['By heating aluminium oxide with coke in a furnace','By electrolysing aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite','By treating cryolite with sodium hydroxide solution under pressure','By heating sodium aluminium silicate to a high temperature'])
no(243,'The first nitrate formula omits X. Even if restored to X(NO₃)₂, both Cu and Pb lie below Fe and their oxides can be reduced by hydrogen under suitable conditions. A is conventional but the two tests do not uniquely distinguish it from lead. Recover additional conditions or a sound original alternative set.',answer='A conventional; D also chemically possible')
yes(244,'B','In electrolytic refining, impure copper is the anode and a sheet of pure copper is the cathode. Copper atoms at the anode lose electrons and enter solution as Cu²⁺. Copper ions gain electrons at the cathode, depositing purer metal. Some impurities remain dissolved and others form anode sludge.',[EL,'https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2020-11/documents/b12s03.pdf'],question='Crude copper can be purified by electrolysis of aqueous copper(II) sulfate when',options=['Platinum electrodes are used','The crude copper is made the anode','The crude copper is made the cathode','Both electrodes are made of crude copper'],audit='Removed trailing O belonging to the next structure. Avoided claiming all impurities settle as sludge.',check='copper-refining')
no(245,'Source structure depicts the four-carbon parent of 2-methylbutanoic acid (A), not old D; carbonyl double bond is also missing. Options B/C are badly corrupted names (hydrosyketone/hydroxyl baldheaded). Need exact original structure and names before publishing, not a guessed nomenclature replacement.',answer='A if carbonyl restored')
yes(246,'B','An alkanoic acid reacts with an alkanol in esterification to form an alkyl alkanoate ester and water. Reaction with sodium instead forms an ionic carboxylate salt, not an alkyl ester.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-3-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-and-esters'],question='Alkyl alkanoate esters are formed by the reaction of alkanoic acids with',options=['Alkyl halides','Alkanols','Ethers','Sodium'],audit='Clarified the ester sense of alkanoates; sodium gives a carboxylate salt, which must not be confused with an alkyl ester.')
yes(247,'B','Alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons are names of classes. Ethane, ethene, ethyne and benzene are individual members, while methane through butane are all members of the alkane class.',[ORG],question='Which list names four classes of hydrocarbons?',options=['Ethane, ethene, ethyne and benzene','Alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons','Alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and benzene','Methane, ethane, propane and butane'],audit='Repaired alkenesm and changed the four classes to four classes, avoiding an exhaustive taxonomy claim.')
no(248,'Source scheme actually prints smaller alkanes plus alkanes plus hydrogen. The missing unsaturated product cannot be restored simply because the old answer is cracking. Need the original product labels; reforming/dehydrogenation cannot be excluded from the damaged scheme.',answer='B intended, products unresolved')
yes(249,'D','Diastase is an enzyme preparation that speeds the hydrolysis of starch into smaller sugars such as maltose. Enzymes act as catalysts: they provide a faster reaction pathway and are regenerated during the catalytic process.',['https://edu.rsc.org/resources/on-this-day-jan-06--anselme-payen-was-born/10106.article','https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis'],question='In the reaction 2(C₆H₁₀O₅)ₙ + nH₂O → nC₁₂H₂₂O₁₁, with diastase over the reaction arrow, diastase functions as',options=['A dehydrating agent','A reducing agent','An oxidising agent','A catalyst'],audit='Restored source arrow, subscripts and diastase label as accessible text. Checked repeat-unit atom balance.',check='starch-hydrolysis')
yes(250,'A','Butan-1-ol has an O–H group, so its molecules form hydrogen bonds with one another. The aldehyde and ether cannot donate hydrogen bonds to their own molecules, and butane is non-polar. For these similarly sized molecules, this makes butan-1-ol the highest-boiling compound.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-1-intermolecular-forces','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/14222?exam_type=jamb&exam_year=1988&page=5'],question='Which compound has the highest boiling point?',options=['CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃','CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃'],audit='Recovered terminal CH₃ in ether option D from matching Myschool 1988 item14222; source PDF had terminal CH₂. A is supported independently by intermolecular forces.')
no(251,'General detergents include anionic, cationic and nonionic classes, while the intended B is a sulfonate. Source A R(CH₂)NOH has an unresolved index/valence and B/C lack anion charges. Need the exact alternatives and an anionic-sulfonate scope before publication; cannot invent A from a likely surfactant class.',answer='B intended sulfonate detergent')
yes(252,'B','Destructive distillation heats coal with air excluded. The coal decomposes into coke and volatile products. Cooling and separating the volatile products yields coal tar and ammoniacal liquor, while coal gas remains gaseous.',[COAL],question='Which process converts coal into coal gas, coal tar, ammoniacal liquor and coke?',options=['Steam distillation','Destructive distillation','Liquefaction','Hydrolysis'],audit='Removed the following 1989 heading from option D and standardised ammoniacal liquor.')
yes(253,'C','When sodium chloride dissolves, its ions disperse through the water. Evaporating the water can recover the salt, so this is classified as a physical change. The other processes involve chemical reactions that produce new substances.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/1-3-physical-and-chemical-properties'],year=1990,question='Which process is classified as a physical change?',options=['Bubbling chlorine into water','Bubbling chlorine into a jar containing hydrogen','Dissolving sodium chloride in water','Passing steam over heated iron'],audit='Actual year1990 confirmed by page27 heading. Corrected sodium chlorine to chloride. Replaced old explanation denying all new interactions with physical-change classification.')
no(254,'Actual1990 page27. Diagram clearly shows X solid→gas, Y gas→liquid, Z liquid→solid, so A (sublimation, condensation, freezing), not old C. Essential arrows are absent in normalized text. Retain held until accessible figure/complete equivalent diagram is supplied.',year=1990,answer='A')
no(255,'Actual1990 page27. SnO₂ molar mass151; 32g requires (32/151)×24/0.80=6.3576g coke, or0.0063576kg. None of0.40kg,0.20kg,0.06kg,0.40g fits. Old explanation uses wrong molar mass and stoichiometry. Need original measurement/options, no nearest choice.',year=1990,answer='6.36g coke; none listed')
yes(256,'C','Using the relative atomic masses Mg = 24 and O = 16, 24 g of Mg contains one mole of atoms. A mole of oxygen molecules has mass 32 g, so it contains the same number of particles. The other choices each contain half a mole of molecules.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/3-1-formula-mass-and-the-mole-concept'],year=1990,question='Which sample contains the same number of molecules as the number of atoms in 24 g of magnesium? [Mg = 24, H = 1, O = 16, Cl = 35.5]',options=['1 g of H₂','16 g of O₂','32 g of O₂','35.5 g of Cl₂'],audit='Actual1990 confirmed from page27. Restored missing g after24 and35.5; expressed the original Avogadro particle comparison clearly.',check='particle-count')
no(257,'Dalton law gives hydrogen pressure760−23=737mmHg. A and D are both printed737mmHg, giving duplicate correct choices. Need a trustworthy original to recover one option rather than silently choosing A over D.',answer='737mmHg, both A and D')
yes(258,'C','Lithium, sodium and potassium occupy successive periods in Group 1. Their occupied shells increase from two to three to four. The extra shells and shielding make the outer electrons farther from the nucleus, explaining the increase in atomic radius.',[PERIODIC],question='The atomic radii of Li, Na and K are given as 1.33 Å, 1.54 Å and 1.96 Å respectively. What best explains the increase?',options=['Electropositivity decreases from Li to Na to K','Electronegativity decreases from Li to Na to K','The number of occupied electron shells increases from Li to Na to K','The elements are in the same period'],audit='Repaired radius punctuation/unit glyphs and Ma→Na. Retained supplied radii as given values rather than asserting one universal radius convention.')
