yes(273,'D','Hess’s law gives ΔH(combustion) = 2ΔHf(CO₂) + 2ΔHf(H₂O) − ΔHf(C₂H₄), since O₂ has zero standard enthalpy of formation. Thus −1428 = 2(−396) + 2(−286) − x, giving x = +64 kJ mol⁻¹ with the supplied data.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-3-enthalpy','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/27577?exam_type=jamb&exam_year=1989&page=4'],question='Ethene burns according to C₂H₄(g) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), with ΔH = −1428 kJ mol⁻¹. Given ΔHf(H₂O(l)) = −286 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔHf(CO₂(g)) = −396 kJ mol⁻¹, calculate ΔHf(C₂H₄(g)) using these data.',options=['−2792 kJ mol⁻¹','+2792 kJ mol⁻¹','−64 kJ mol⁻¹','+64 kJ mol⁻¹'],audit='Matching Myschool27577 confirms ethene C₂H₄, oxygen reactant and carbon(IV)oxide; source PDF conflicts ethene/C₂H₂/ethane. Preserved supplied thermochemical values and qualified calculated answer as using those data.',check='hess-ethene')
yes(274,'D','There are two moles of gas on each side, so changing pressure does not favour either side in the ideal-gas model. Because the forward reaction is exothermic, higher temperature favours the reverse direction. Adding excess steam shifts equilibrium toward CO₂ and H₂, so only IV favours hydrogen formation.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle'],question='For CO(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) + H₂(g), ΔH = −41 kJ mol⁻¹. Which listed changes favour hydrogen formation at equilibrium? I: higher pressure; II: lower pressure; III: higher temperature; IV: excess steam.',options=['I, III and IV','III only','II, III and I','IV only'],audit='Restored water vapour state from explicit steam condition and equilibrium arrow from PDF; converted41000J to41kJ without changing magnitude.',check='water-gas-equilibrium')
no(275,'Essential heating curve is absent. Visual first plateau is labelledY and later sloping segmentU, so source suggests D rather than oldB. Need high-resolution confirmation of all labels and accessible figure before publishing; no generic assumption that U is the melting plateau.',answer='D provisional visual first-plateau label')
yes(276,'B','Concentrated nitric acid oxidises copper to Cu²⁺ while nitrate is reduced mainly to NO₂. The balanced equation is Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O + 2NO₂. Equation C describes the familiar dilute-acid reaction producing NO instead.',['https://teachchemistry.org/classroom-resources/nitric-acid-acts-upon-copper',MATCH],question='Which equation describes the reaction of copper with concentrated nitric acid?',options=['Cu + 2HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + H₂','Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O + 2NO₂','3Cu + 8HNO₃ → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 4H₂O + 2NO','3Cu + 4HNO₃ → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O + 2NO'],audit='Recovered missingCu and HNO₃ ordering inA from matching Myschool1989 page5. Restored arrows and notation. D remains deliberately unbalanced; B/C distinction is acid concentration.',check='copper-nitric')
yes(277,'C','Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅, catalyses the oxidation of SO₂ to SO₃ in the contact process. The SO₃ is then used to produce sulfuric acid. The catalyst increases the reaction rate without changing the equilibrium position.',['https://edu.rsc.org/download?ac=12434',MATCH],question='Which catalyst is used in the contact process for manufacturing sulfuric acid?',options=['Manganese(IV) oxide','Manganese(II) sulfate','Vanadium(V) oxide','Iron metal'],audit='Matching Myschool1989 page5 confirms sulfate(VI) inB where PDF said tetraoxosulfate(IV). Converted equivalent acid/salt names.')
yes(278,'D','Heating coal without air produces coke as the solid residue. Volatile products include ammonia, which dissolves in condensed water to form ammoniacal liquor. Coal tar and coal gas are other products of this process.',[COAL],question='Which pair consists of products of the destructive distillation of coal?',options=['Carbon dioxide and ethanoic acid','Carbonic acid and methanoic acid','Producer gas and water gas','Coke and ammoniacal liquor'],audit='Standardised equivalent compound names and ammoniacal-liquor spelling.')
yes(279,'A','Potassium nitrate acts as the oxidising agent. It supports oxidation of the combustible components rather than acting as a solvent or as a catalyst. An oxidant is reduced while causing another substance to be oxidised.',['https://edu.rsc.org/experiments/thermal-decomposition-of-nitrates-writing-with-fire/712.article'],question='In gunpowder containing charcoal, sulfur and potassium nitrate, what is the role of potassium nitrate?',options=['An oxidant','A reductant','A solvent','A catalyst'],audit='Corrected nitrate spelling and removed page marker. Kept answer conceptual with no preparation instructions or proportions.')
yes(280,'C','Carbon dioxide in moist air reacts with bleaching powder, leading to loss of available chlorine. This lowers its bleaching strength during exposure. Atmospheric nitrogen does not displace chlorine in this way.',['https://www.bhu.ac.in/research_pub/jsr/Volumes/JSR_65_06_2021/Manuscript%2030.pdf'],question='Bleaching powder deteriorates on exposure to air mainly because',options=['It loses its water of crystallisation','Atmospheric nitrogen displaces chlorine from it','Atmospheric carbon dioxide reacts with it and releases chlorine','Bleaching agents should be stored in solution'],audit='Removed simplistic hydrate formula from stem because commercial bleaching powder is a mixture; retained the question and sourceC reaction mechanism without claiming a pure stoichiometric material.')
